course: dictionary
lesson: 11715
title: Words that signal good things ahead
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Auspicious is a formal word, the kind you meet in speeches, stories and exam writing. It means showing signs that something will go well. Writers use it when things look hopeful, like a lucky start.
explain: Look at the clues around it. «The sun came out just as the wedding began, an auspicious start to the day.» Sunshine, a happy event and the word start all point to good things. So auspicious must mean promising or lucky.
explain: Auspicious can also mean special and important, as in «on this auspicious occasion». Many people choose an auspicious day for big moments like weddings or new beginnings. Phrases to remember are «an auspicious start», «an auspicious day» and «hardly auspicious».
fact: Auspicious means showing signs that something will go well.
fact: Auspicious is a formal word, so you will meet it in speeches and stories.
fact: «An auspicious start» means a promising, lucky beginning.
fact: «Hardly auspicious» means the signs look bad, not good.
fact: In some cultures, people choose an auspicious day for weddings or new starts.
exercise: «The sun came out just as the wedding began: what an auspicious start!» What does auspicious mean here? | A cloudy, grey sky | A loud noise | *A promising sign that things will go well | A sad, slow ending
exercise: Mum says, «The bus has broken down again, hardly auspicious for our trip.» What does hardly auspicious suggest? | The trip will be very lucky | *The signs look bad for the trip | Everyone is cheering | The bus is brand new
exercise: Find and copy the word that means special and important: «They chose an auspicious day for the school opening.» | day | opening | chose | *auspicious
test: «The new school year began on an auspicious day, with blue skies and no rain.» What does auspicious suggest? | *Signs that the year will go well | Something that is over | Cold and wet weather | A day nobody will remember
test: Which word is closest in meaning to auspicious when it means promising? | Sleepy | Gloomy | *Hopeful | Rude
exam: Which sentence uses auspicious correctly? | The dog ran auspicious across the field | My auspicious pencil case is red | I ate an auspicious sandwich for lunch | *The bright sunshine was an auspicious sign for our sports day
